1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
On a calm spring afternoon in 1915, a single

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:07,001
torpedo struck the most famous liner on the Atlantic.

3
00:00:07,041 --> 00:00:09,522
Eighteen minutes later, she was gone, and

4
00:00:09,562 --> 00:00:12,043
nearly twelve hundred people had died with her.

5
00:00:12,083 --> 00:00:14,064
One hundred and twenty-eight of the dead were

6
00:00:14,104 --> 00:00:16,085
citizens of a country not even at war.

7
00:00:16,125 --> 00:00:20,126
Here's how the sinking of the Lusitania turned America against Germany.

8
00:00:20,166 --> 00:00:22,147
And why it still took two more

9
00:00:22,187 --> 00:00:24,168
years before American soldiers went to war.

10
00:00:24,208 --> 00:00:27,189
When war broke out in Europe in August

11
00:00:27,229 --> 00:00:30,210
1914, the United States chose to stay out.

12
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:32,730
President Woodrow Wilson asked Americans to be

13
00:00:32,770 --> 00:00:35,251
neutral in thought as well as action.

14
00:00:35,291 --> 00:00:38,272
Millions of Americans had roots in Germany, Ireland

15
00:00:38,312 --> 00:00:41,293
and Britain, and few wanted another country's war.

16
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:43,814
Yet American factories and banks were

17
00:00:43,854 --> 00:00:46,335
already trading heavily with Britain and France.

18
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:48,855
Britain's navy blockaded Germany, choking the flow

19
00:00:48,895 --> 00:00:51,376
of food and raw materials to its ports.

20
00:00:51,416 --> 00:00:53,397
Germany answered with a new kind of

21
00:00:53,437 --> 00:00:55,418
weapon that struck from beneath the waves.

22
00:00:55,458 --> 00:01:01,460
In February 1915, Germany declared the waters around Britain a war zone.

23
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,480
Enemy merchant ships in that zone could now

24
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:07,501
be sunk without warning, and neutrals sailed at risk.

25
00:01:07,541 --> 00:01:10,022
Wilson replied that Germany would be held

26
00:01:10,062 --> 00:01:12,543
to strict accountability for any American lives lost.

27
00:01:12,583 --> 00:01:15,564
In March, an American named Leon Thrasher drowned

28
00:01:15,604 --> 00:01:18,585
when a U-boat sank the British ship Falaba.

29
00:01:18,625 --> 00:01:21,605
Then, on May 1, a U-boat torpedoed

30
00:01:21,645 --> 00:01:24,626
the American tanker Gulflight, and three men died.

31
00:01:24,666 --> 00:01:27,147
At the centre of this danger sailed

32
00:01:27,187 --> 00:01:29,668
the Lusitania, pride of the Cunard line.

33
00:01:29,708 --> 00:01:33,189
When she entered service in 1907, she was

34
00:01:33,229 --> 00:01:36,710
one of the largest and fastest ships ever built.

35
00:01:36,750 --> 00:01:40,230
In 1907 she won the Blue Riband for

36
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:43,751
the fastest Atlantic crossing, taking the title from Germany.

37
00:01:43,791 --> 00:01:48,793
Her passengers included millionaires, families, nannies and children, all bound for Liverpool.

38
00:01:48,833 --> 00:01:53,835
On the morning she sailed, a strange notice appeared in American newspapers.

39
00:01:53,875 --> 00:01:56,355
The German embassy warned that travellers on

40
00:01:56,395 --> 00:01:58,876
British ships sailed at their own risk.

41
00:01:58,916 --> 00:02:01,397
In some papers, it was printed

42
00:02:01,437 --> 00:02:03,918
right beside Cunard's advertisement for the Lusitania.

43
00:02:03,958 --> 00:02:06,439
Almost nobody cancelled, because no one believed

44
00:02:06,479 --> 00:02:08,960
Germany would sink a ship full of civilians.

45
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,480
On May 1, 1915, the Lusitania left

46
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:16,001
New York with nearly two thousand people aboard.

47
00:02:16,041 --> 00:02:21,043
Commanding her was Captain William Turner, one of Cunard's most experienced captains.

48
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:23,564
To save coal, one of her four boiler

49
00:02:23,604 --> 00:02:26,085
rooms had been shut down, cutting her top speed.

50
00:02:26,125 --> 00:02:31,126
Meanwhile, off the south coast of Ireland, a German submarine was hunting.

51
00:02:31,166 --> 00:02:33,647
The liner was just eleven miles off

52
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:36,168
the Irish coast, almost within sight of safety.

53
00:02:36,208 --> 00:02:38,689
U-20 was commanded by Walther Schwieger, a young

54
00:02:38,729 --> 00:02:41,210
officer who had already sunk several ships that week.

55
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:43,730
On May 7, Turner slowed his ship in thick

56
00:02:43,770 --> 00:02:46,251
fog as he neared the Old Head of Kinsale.

57
00:02:46,291 --> 00:02:51,293
Through his periscope, Schwieger saw four funnels rising above the horizon.

58
00:02:51,333 --> 00:02:53,814
At ten minutes past two in

59
00:02:53,854 --> 00:02:56,335
the afternoon, he fired a single torpedo.

60
00:02:56,375 --> 00:02:59,376
It struck the starboard side, just behind the bridge.

61
00:02:59,416 --> 00:03:01,897
Seconds later, a second, much larger

62
00:03:01,937 --> 00:03:04,418
explosion tore through the ship from inside.

63
00:03:04,458 --> 00:03:07,439
In his war diary, Schwieger noted an unusually

64
00:03:07,479 --> 00:03:10,460
heavy explosion and a huge cloud of smoke.

65
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:12,980
Historians still argue about that second blast, from

66
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,501
boilers and steam to coal dust or cargo.

67
00:03:15,541 --> 00:03:18,022
The Lusitania lurched hard to starboard,

68
00:03:18,062 --> 00:03:20,543
and water poured into her open portholes.

69
00:03:20,583 --> 00:03:24,585
Her steep tilt made most lifeboats impossible to lower safely.

70
00:03:24,625 --> 00:03:28,626
Of her forty-eight lifeboats, only six were successfully lowered.

71
00:03:28,666 --> 00:03:31,147
Passengers jumped into the cold water,

72
00:03:31,187 --> 00:03:33,668
clinging to deck chairs, oars and wreckage.

73
00:03:33,708 --> 00:03:36,189
Just eighteen minutes after the torpedo struck,

74
00:03:36,229 --> 00:03:38,710
the great ship slipped beneath the sea.

75
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:42,751
Schwieger did not stay to help, and U-20 slipped away toward home.

76
00:03:42,791 --> 00:03:45,272
Fishing boats and steamers rushed from the

77
00:03:45,312 --> 00:03:47,793
coast to pull survivors from the water.

78
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:51,835
Survivors and the dead were brought to the Irish port of Queenstown.

79
00:03:51,875 --> 00:03:55,876
Nearly twelve hundred people were lost, including ninety-four children.

80
00:03:55,916 --> 00:03:59,918
Only around seven hundred and sixty passengers and crew survived.

81
00:03:59,958 --> 00:04:03,960
Within hours, the news flashed across the Atlantic by telegraph.

82
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,001
American newspapers filled their front pages with the names of the dead.

83
00:04:08,041 --> 00:04:11,022
Among them were famous Americans, like the

84
00:04:11,062 --> 00:04:14,043
millionaire Alfred Vanderbilt and the producer Charles Frohman.

85
00:04:14,083 --> 00:04:16,564
Of the one hundred and fifty-nine Americans

86
00:04:16,604 --> 00:04:19,085
aboard, one hundred and twenty-eight never came home.

87
00:04:19,125 --> 00:04:21,605
Crowds gathered outside newspaper offices in New

88
00:04:21,645 --> 00:04:24,126
York, waiting for each new list of names.

89
00:04:24,166 --> 00:04:29,168
Former president Theodore Roosevelt called the attack piracy and demanded action.

90
00:04:29,208 --> 00:04:31,689
Editorials called it murder on the

91
00:04:31,729 --> 00:04:34,210
high seas and a crime against civilisation.

92
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:36,730
Germany insisted that the Lusitania was

93
00:04:36,770 --> 00:04:39,251
a legitimate target carrying weapons for Britain.

94
00:04:39,291 --> 00:04:42,272
Berlin even claimed the ship was armed,

95
00:04:42,312 --> 00:04:45,293
but American inspectors had found no guns aboard.

96
00:04:45,333 --> 00:04:48,314
Her cargo did include millions of rifle cartridges

97
00:04:48,354 --> 00:04:51,335
and over a thousand cases of shrapnel shells.

98
00:04:51,375 --> 00:04:53,355
That cargo was listed openly on the

99
00:04:53,395 --> 00:04:55,376
ship’s manifest when she left New York.

100
00:04:55,416 --> 00:04:57,897
But to most Americans, that could never

101
00:04:57,937 --> 00:05:00,418
justify drowning women and children without warning.

102
00:05:00,458 --> 00:05:03,439
For the first time, many ordinary Americans saw Germany

103
00:05:03,479 --> 00:05:06,460
not as a distant power, but as a threat.

104
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:10,501
Woodrow Wilson now faced the hardest choice of his presidency.

105
00:05:10,541 --> 00:05:13,522
Three days later, he told a Philadelphia audience

106
00:05:13,562 --> 00:05:16,543
that a nation could be too proud to fight.

107
00:05:16,583 --> 00:05:19,064
The phrase was mocked by critics, who saw

108
00:05:19,104 --> 00:05:21,585
it as weakness in the face of murder.

109
00:05:21,625 --> 00:05:26,626
Instead of war, Wilson sent Germany a series of sharp diplomatic notes.

110
00:05:26,666 --> 00:05:31,668
The first, in May, demanded that Germany stop attacking unarmed passenger ships.

111
00:05:31,708 --> 00:05:34,189
His Secretary of State, William Jennings Bryan,

112
00:05:34,229 --> 00:05:36,710
feared the notes would drag America into war.

113
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:39,730
America's ambassador in London warned that the

114
00:05:39,770 --> 00:05:42,751
country must declare war or lose Europe's respect.

115
00:05:42,791 --> 00:05:46,793
On June 9, rather than sign the second note, Bryan resigned.

116
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:50,835
His replacement, Robert Lansing, was far more sympathetic to Britain.

117
00:05:50,875 --> 00:05:53,355
A third note in July warned that

118
00:05:53,395 --> 00:05:55,876
further attacks would be seen as deliberately unfriendly.

119
00:05:55,916 --> 00:05:57,897
Germany's replies defended the sinking and

120
00:05:57,937 --> 00:05:59,918
refused to admit it had been illegal.

121
00:05:59,958 --> 00:06:02,439
Wilson was walking a tightrope between public anger

122
00:06:02,479 --> 00:06:04,960
and his promise to keep America at peace.

123
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,480
Britain understood the power of the tragedy,

124
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:10,001
and quickly turned it into a weapon.

125
00:06:10,041 --> 00:06:12,522
In Germany, an artist named Karl Goetz

126
00:06:12,562 --> 00:06:15,043
made a satirical medal mocking the sinking.

127
00:06:15,083 --> 00:06:17,564
It was dated May the fifth, two days early,

128
00:06:17,604 --> 00:06:20,085
and Britain called that error proof of a plan.

129
00:06:20,125 --> 00:06:22,605
British officials made huge numbers of copies

130
00:06:22,645 --> 00:06:25,126
and sold them as proof of German cruelty.

131
00:06:25,166 --> 00:06:28,147
Around three hundred thousand copies were sold, raising

132
00:06:28,187 --> 00:06:31,168
money for the Red Cross and blinded soldiers.

133
00:06:31,208 --> 00:06:33,689
Days after the sinking, Britain also published

134
00:06:33,729 --> 00:06:36,210
the Bryce Report on German atrocities in Belgium.

135
00:06:36,250 --> 00:06:39,730
Many of its stories were later shown to

136
00:06:39,770 --> 00:06:43,251
be exaggerated, but in 1915 they were widely believed.

137
00:06:43,291 --> 00:06:46,272
In America, a famous recruiting poster showed a

138
00:06:46,312 --> 00:06:49,293
drowning mother clutching her baby beneath one word: Enlist.

139
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:52,314
The Lusitania became a symbol of a war

140
00:06:52,354 --> 00:06:55,335
being fought not just by armies, but against civilians.

141
00:06:55,375 --> 00:06:57,855
Anger over the sinking also boosted a

142
00:06:57,895 --> 00:07:00,376
Preparedness Movement demanding a bigger American army.

143
00:07:00,416 --> 00:07:02,897
That summer, volunteers began training as officers

144
00:07:02,937 --> 00:07:05,418
at a camp in Plattsburgh, New York.

145
00:07:05,458 --> 00:07:07,939
In Berlin, German leaders realised they could

146
00:07:07,979 --> 00:07:10,460
not afford to push America any further.

147
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:13,480
Germany's admirals were furious, convinced their submarines

148
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:16,501
could win the war if they were unleashed.

149
00:07:16,541 --> 00:07:20,022
But in August 1915, another U-boat sank

150
00:07:20,062 --> 00:07:23,543
the British liner Arabic, and more Americans died.

151
00:07:23,583 --> 00:07:26,564
Facing a break with Washington, Germany promised

152
00:07:26,604 --> 00:07:29,585
not to sink passenger liners without warning.

153
00:07:29,625 --> 00:07:33,605
The German ambassador, Count Johann von Bernstorff,

154
00:07:33,645 --> 00:07:37,626
delivered this Arabic pledge in September 1915.

155
00:07:37,666 --> 00:07:41,147
In March 1916, a torpedo hit the

156
00:07:41,187 --> 00:07:44,668
French ferry Sussex, injuring several Americans aboard.

157
00:07:44,708 --> 00:07:49,710
Around eighty people died, among them the Spanish composer Enrique Granados.

158
00:07:49,750 --> 00:07:52,730
Wilson threatened to cut diplomatic ties, and

159
00:07:52,770 --> 00:07:55,751
Germany promised again to restrain its submarines.

160
00:07:55,791 --> 00:07:58,772
For the rest of 1916, the U-boats held

161
00:07:58,812 --> 00:08:01,793
back, and America stayed out of the war.

162
00:08:01,833 --> 00:08:04,814
In 1916, Wilson won re-election with a

163
00:08:04,854 --> 00:08:07,835
simple slogan: He kept us out of war.

164
00:08:07,875 --> 00:08:10,355
Yet beneath that peace, American trade, loans

165
00:08:10,395 --> 00:08:12,876
and sympathy were flowing steadily toward Britain.

166
00:08:12,916 --> 00:08:16,397
By early 1917, Germany's generals believed they

167
00:08:16,437 --> 00:08:19,918
could starve Britain before America could act.

168
00:08:19,958 --> 00:08:25,960
On February 1, Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare across the Atlantic.

169
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,480
Germany knew this would probably bring America

170
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:31,001
in, and gambled that it would not matter.

171
00:08:31,041 --> 00:08:35,043
Two days later, Wilson broke off diplomatic relations with Germany.

172
00:08:35,083 --> 00:08:40,085
Then British codebreakers revealed a secret German message called the Zimmermann Telegram.

173
00:08:40,125 --> 00:08:43,105
It offered Mexico Texas, New Mexico and Arizona

174
00:08:43,145 --> 00:08:46,126
if it joined a war against the United States.

175
00:08:46,166 --> 00:08:48,647
Britain had decoded it in January, and

176
00:08:48,687 --> 00:08:51,168
handed it to Washington on February 24.

177
00:08:51,208 --> 00:08:56,210
When the telegram was published on March 1, Americans were outraged.

178
00:08:56,250 --> 00:08:59,230
In March, U-boats sank several American merchant

179
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:02,251
ships, and public patience finally ran out.

180
00:09:02,291 --> 00:09:05,272
On April 2, 1917, Wilson asked Congress

181
00:09:05,312 --> 00:09:08,293
for a declaration of war against Germany.

182
00:09:08,333 --> 00:09:12,335
He told them the world must be made safe for democracy.

183
00:09:12,375 --> 00:09:16,376
Four days later, on April 6, Congress voted for war.

184
00:09:16,416 --> 00:09:18,897
The Senate voted eighty-two to six, and

185
00:09:18,937 --> 00:09:21,418
the House three hundred seventy-three to fifty.

186
00:09:21,458 --> 00:09:23,939
As American soldiers sailed for France, the

187
00:09:23,979 --> 00:09:26,460
memory of the Lusitania sailed with them.

188
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:30,501
The Lusitania did not take America to war on its own.

189
00:09:30,541 --> 00:09:33,522
That took two years, broken promises, a secret telegram

190
00:09:33,562 --> 00:09:36,543
and more ships at the bottom of the sea.

191
00:09:36,583 --> 00:09:38,564
But it was the moment millions of

192
00:09:38,604 --> 00:09:40,585
Americans first saw Germany as an enemy.

193
00:09:40,625 --> 00:09:47,626
In 1925, Germany finally paid compensation to the families of American victims.

194
00:09:47,666 --> 00:09:50,647
Today, the wreck still lies on the seabed off

195
00:09:50,687 --> 00:09:53,668
the Irish coast, nearly a hundred metres below the waves.

196
00:09:53,708 --> 00:09:56,689
The Lusitania did not drag America into the war,

197
00:09:56,729 --> 00:09:59,710
but it decided which side America would fight on.
